ReadMe for the Tubi Interview File. 

NOTE: All code was written by me unless specified by a stackexchange link.

NOTE: Unforunately this code is no longer runable due to access changes in the X / Twitter API, however, the Jupyter notebook file has a 
sample output of the API and the image files provide a sample output of the visualizations. 

This will describe the Jupyter notebook and the functions within for the Emerging Geopolitical Topics system I developed with machine learning 
that takes in X / Twitter streaming data and creates topics over time. The two image files are the output, the Iran_Topic is a sample output 
of the Jupyter notebook and the Iran_Google_Trends is the comparsion to the X / Twitter emerging topics.

Part 1: The incoming streaming data from X / Twitter
Functions:
bearer_oauth - Method required by bearer token authentication
get_rules - Initializes the rules to be added to collect the incoming streaming data
delete_all_rules - Delete all the Rules Function, to clean the slate
set_rules - this is the set of rules to be listened for, in my case this was variation geopolitical topics across 7 languages, it was also
possible to add "tags" to help identify the specific topics later on
Merge - a helper function to merge dictionaries called in the get_stream function
get_stream - Get Stream API Started Function, get the requests of the stream with the rules and the created_at data as well, as the data
comes in one dictionary is created to hold the text data, created_at, and other dictionary is created to hold the tag, after 100 tweets 
are collected the file is outputted into a json file and stored, then data is reset for next set of collection
main_2 - combines the function for running afterwards, there is also a sample out of the tweets collected at the bottom

Part 2: Emerging Topics over Time (Only English)
For sake of time, I will only go through the English topics here, after an hour of data collection, the first run of the topic modeling 
occurs.
Functions:
all_files - Gets all files from Json_file output, import the jsons and convert them to pandas dataframes, concat the individual data 
frames into one dataframe
lang_detect_2 - helper function for calling the langdetect library 
lang_loop - detects the language of each tweet and labels the data with the language
English_Tweets_Func - get only the English tweets
Functions date_func through sort_chrono, are time helper functions 
stopwords_en_func - creates the English stop words
stopwords_en - removes the stop words from the texts
bigrams - makes bigrams (two-word phrases) from the non stop word texts
data_lemmatization_en - Turn the words into lemmas English (Cutting off the endings of words (ex: -ed, -ing) for better groupings
createList - a helper function for creating topic_id numbers, change dynamically
compute_coherence_values - Detour to focus on hyperparameter tunning of the LDA Model, first build the function to test the 
hyperparameters of the number of topics (k), the document - topic density (alpha) and the Word - topic density (beta), we get the 
coherence score for each model using the 'c_v' which is measure is based on a sliding window, one-set segmentation of the top words
and an indirect confirmation measure that uses normalized pointwise mutual information (NPMI) and the cosine similarity
lda_hyperparameter_generating_en - LDA Hyperparameter Finding function for English, loops through various combinations of hyperparameters
of lda to find the maximum coherence value
lda_hyper_define_en - Hyper Parameter Defining for English, getting the max coherence across the different amounts of topics then 
getting the hyperparameters based on the max coherence value, (coherence is a value of topic interprebility, a measure of 
semantic similarity
build_lda_en - using both the hyperparameters idenified and the stopwords, bigram and lemma function to get the final LDA model. 
Then create the weights dataframe, extract individual document topic proportions as determined by the LDA model.
The Gensim LDA model can classify, the specific relative proportions for all ten topics within each document as long as you set the
minimum_probability, argument to 0. If not dine, then some topics may be dropped from the final weighting if they did not 
meet the probability threshold set by default. Extraction Loop: This loop extracts the topic proportions for all topics for
every individual document and places them into a dataframe with a document-id key for merging topic proportion information 
with other datasets about our corpus. The final output of this function is a dataframe that has the final per-document dataframe over time 
which is then used for visualizations. 
viz_topic_time_en - Visualization of Topics over Time English
main_tm - putting it all together

Lastly - the two output images show the difference between this method and a similiar emerging topic tracker, Google Trends, as the images 
show, my method here was able to identify Iran as an emerging topic three hours before it spiked in the Google Trends data, providing 
a longer lead time for decision makers to respond to rapidly evolving geopolitical events. 




